stuffed owls dressed like hipsters are  Rebecca sailors thing she spends five  hours sewing and decorating each one by  hand  sailor sells them for $50 apiece on Etsy  the online marketplace with more than 1  million shops for handmade products Etsy  was the perfect platform for me they  were doing their part to gain customers  and elevate the experience of handmade  that is she says until Etsy became more  corporate they did make some changes  which caused a weird shift in the  ecosystem 16 months ago Etsy allowed  sellers to use manufacturers to produce  on mass the goal was to help sellers  grow their businesses while Etsy built  greater scale Sailor sales have slipped  15% since then the new policy has made  it harder for consumers to find her  pillows and compete on price so why not  just use a manufacturer to compete Etsy  sellers they have this extreme emotional  connection to their piece and they want  the public at large to feel the same way  which they do I'm sure in many ways but  at the end of the day it is a business  Etsy was founded a decade ago by Rob  Kalin and two friends the idea gained  traction with other artisans and caught  the attention of big-name venture  capitalists such as accel partners and  Union Square Ventures over time though  success bred conflict and Kalyn rejected  any stray from the company's core values  and left in 2011 now sources say the  company is preparing for a 300 million  dollar IPO the new policies could help  it look more attractive to Wall Street  Etsy charges 20 cents for each listing  and three point five percent commission  from each sale the more transactions the  better Etsy declined to comment on the  policy changes in the IPO Kailyn did not  respond to requests seeking comment but  these changes are common in the lead up  to a public offering even if it means  offending some customers you know  companies need to evolve those that  don't risk being left behind in a world  around them and when they tap out their  market I think the trick is to do it in  a gradual way or way that doesn't offend  your original base as if a company moves  too quickly it can have negative impact  on the current customer base and that of  course shows up in terms of revenues and  margins and all of those things at an  Etsy team meetup in San Francisco  sellers have mixed reactions to the IPO  some say these changes are just the  price you pay for being part of a larger  ecosystem but Rick Kitagawa left the  site when the policy changes were  announced for a site that kind of clings  to really empower crafters I didn't  really feel that it was actually doing  that and yet some see Etsy's IPO as an  opportunity they've started a campaign  to raise 1.5 million and build a stake  in the IPO those used to expressing  themselves through art are starting to  do so with their wallets
 

